{"step_index":251,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-01T09:15:13Z","thinking":"The `prepare_statement` function appears to parse an input string to determine a statement type. It checks if the input starts with \"insert\" to identify an insertion statement. If the input is exactly \"select\", it likely sets up a selection statement. Further checks would be needed to handle other statement types or malformed inputs.\n\n\nAnalyzing `prepare_statement`, the function returns a boolean indicating success or failure of statement preparation. The input string is passed as a constant reference to avoid copying, while `Statement` is passed as a non-constant reference, enabling modification of the caller's `statement` object. The function employs `rfind` to check if input starts with \"insert\", and if so, it sets the statement type to `STATEMENT_INSERT`. A direct comparison is used for \"select\".\n\nIf the input is \"select,\" the function sets the statement type to `STATEMENT_SELECT`. Otherwise, the function implicitly assumes that if the input doesn't start with insert or is not equal to select, it's an unrecognized statement and returns `false`. This design covers simple cases but lacks robust error handling for unsupported statement types.\n\n\nIf the input is \"select,\" the `statement.type` is set, and preparation is considered successful; otherwise, it's flagged as unrecognized and returns a failure. Updated the documentation to include `.rfind()`, pass-by-reference (`&`), and `const`. These updates should clarify the logic and coding patterns used in the `prepare_statement` function.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"\"# C++ & Database Study Notes (Cheat Sheet)\\n\\nThese notes explain every new term and concept we encounter as we build our database.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. C++ Keywords & Concepts\\n\\n### Line-by-Line Breakdown: `prepare_statement`\\n\\n```cpp\\nbool prepare_statement(const string& input, Statement& statement) {\\n```\\n\\n1. **`bool` (Return Type):**\\n   - The function finishes by reporting **`true`** (Success: command understood) or **`false`** (Failure: unrecognized command).\\n2. **`const string& input`:**\\n   - **`string input`:** The text the user typed into the terminal.\\n   - **`const` (Constant / Read-Only):** A safety promise that this function will only *read* the user's text, never alter or delete it.\\n   - **`&` (Reference / No-Copy):** Tells C++ not to waste time duplicating the string in RAM. It looks directly at the original variable.\\n3. **`Statement& statement`:**\\n   - **Why NO `const`?** Because the function's entire job is to **fill in and modify** the `statement` box!\\n   - **Why `&`?** Ensures the changes are made directly to the original `statement` ticket in `main()`, not a temporary copy.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### How to Check if a String Starts with a Word: `.rfind(\\\"insert\\\", 0) == 0`\\n- **Why can't we use `input == \\\"insert\\\"`?**\\n  Because the user types arguments after it, like `\\\"insert 1 milk\\\"`. `\\\"insert 1 milk\\\"` does **not** equal `\\\"insert\\\"`.\\n- **What `.rfind(text, 0)` does:**\\n  Searches if the string starts with `text` at position `0` (the very beginning).\\n  - If it starts with `\\\"insert\\\"` at position 0 $\\\\rightarrow$ returns `0`.\\n  - If it does NOT start with `\\\"insert\\\"` $\\\\rightarrow$ returns a huge error number (`string::npos`).\\n- **Meaning of `input.rfind(\\\"insert\\\", 0) == 0`:**\\n  *\\\"Does this sentence start with the word 'insert' at position 0?\\\"*\\n\\n---\\n\\n### The Golden Definition of a `struct`:\\n> **\\\"A `struct` creates a brand new data type that can hold other data types inside it (like `int`, `string`, `double`, `bool`, or even other custom types!).\\\"**\\n\\nLook at how custom structs\n<truncated 3769 bytes>","Description":"\"Add in-depth explanation of prepare_statement and .rfind to NOTES.md\"","Overwrite":"true","Target